ef 17592186064245 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050929 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186064246 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050929 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186064274 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050929 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428935 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050929 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428936 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050929 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428937 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050929 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428938 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050929 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428939 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050929 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428940 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050929 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428941 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050929 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428942 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050929 #edb/ref 1031 "Introduction to Integration by Parts" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050929 #edb/ref 1033 317 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050929 #edb/ref 1034 1.4476087343369344 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050929 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "7280f087-059d-5524-8f8b-a1fe79b4ef9d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050989 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186166960 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050989 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186166961 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050989 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428955 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050989 #edb/ref 1031 "Convergence of Fourier Series" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050989 #edb/ref 1033 3198 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050989 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "7742668d-ad15-5c25-bc82-4ea5b4fd800d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186164819 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186539484 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186164819 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186539506 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186164819 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186539516 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186164819 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186539719 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186164819 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186539760 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186164819 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186164819 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186164819 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186539484 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186164819 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "163adf1f-a5ba-53ac-95f1-d113394667f0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186164929 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186427748 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186164929 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186427749 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186164929 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186427750 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186164929 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186427751 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186164929 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186427752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186164929 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186164929 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186164929 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186427752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186164929 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "32a95669-d006-58f5-a342-6964952c273c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166567 #edb/ref 1069 "e-21809" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166567 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $F(x)=x^2+10x$ for $x\\in[0,6),$ and let $f(x)$ be the **odd** extension of $F(x)$ to $x \\in (-6,6).$ What is the value of $f(-1)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166567 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f7af1dba-6300-56ab-93d2-0f3d1b893dad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166567 #edb/ref 1074 "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in[0,6),$ the odd extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n![](images/de/de535d3df741309e51bab7305aedcb27507b078771f0931d4914e16c88220926.svg)\n\nIn our case, for $x\\in(-6,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =-F(-x) \\\\  & =-((-x)^{2}+10(-x)) \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}-10x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}+10x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =-(-1)^{2}+10(-1)=-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166568 #edb/ref 1069 "e-21810" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166568 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $F(x)=4x$ for $x\\in[0,\\pi).$ Find the missing parts in the Fourier sine series of the **odd** $2\\pi$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of the function $F(x)$ below.\n\n![](images/e2/e25e66141569f5324759eb15a1dcf8b143f5018cf35e957f7a212ba3b45b3556.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166568 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1ff70a32-336a-5ff2-bfe8-0df06eec1a7c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166568 #edb/ref 1074 "For our Fourier sine series, the coefficients are given by\n\n$$\nb_n=\\frac{2}{\\pi}\\int_0^\\pi f(x)\\sin(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=4x$ for $x \\in (0,\\pi).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\nb_n = \\dfrac{8}{\\pi}\\int_0^\\pi x \\sin(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\, & ⇒\\,v=-\\frac{1}{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =\\frac{8}{\\pi }(uv\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{\\pi }(-\\frac{1}{n}[x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }+\\frac{1}{n}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{n\\pi }((\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))-\\frac{1}{n}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }) \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{n\\pi }(\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-\\frac{1}{n}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =-\\frac{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )}{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $\\cos(n\\pi)=(-1)^n,$ we have\n\n$$\nb_n = -\\dfrac{8(-1)^n}{n}=\\dfrac{8(-1)^{n+1}}{n}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the Fourier sine series is\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{8(-1)^{n+1}}{n} \\cdot \\sin(nx).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166955 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049873 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166955 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186167270 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166955 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186167271 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166955 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186315028 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166955 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186315029 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166955 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186315030 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166955 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186315064 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166955 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186315093 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166955 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186379568 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166955 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186379595 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166955 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540067 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166955 #edb/ref 1039 "Understanding the Odd Extension of a Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166955 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186166567 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166955 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "f5ee3c3f-4289-5e38-a47a-52e5c2ad3817" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166956 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050730 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166956 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050929 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166956 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186167283 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166956 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186167284 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166956 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186315033 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166956 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186315034 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166956 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186315083 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166956 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186315087 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166956 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186379575 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166956 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186379582 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166956 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186379589 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166956 #edb/ref 1039 "Computing the Sine Coefficients From the Half-Range Formula" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166956 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186166568 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166956 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "1a85c9c4-8563-55e0-9d4f-7d61d3be6243" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166991 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167931 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166991 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166991 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167933 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166991 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166991 #edb/ref 1045 "Fourier Sine Series" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166991 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186167931 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166991 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048833 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166991 #edb/ref 1050 600.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166991 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "92b5fdfc-2225-50d5-b6fe-9d76c920db9c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167270 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164819 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167270 #edb/ref 1069 "q-338784" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167270 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167270 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $F(x)={x}^{2}-2x$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ and let $f(x)$ be the **odd** extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ What is $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167270 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "487df78e-b6e4-5124-9586-cb2ed967b312" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167270 #edb/ref 1074 "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ the odd extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}F(x), & x\\in [0,\\pi ), \\\\ -F(-x), & x\\in (-\\pi ,0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =-F(-x) \\\\  & =-((-x)^{2}-2(-x)) \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+2x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167284 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164929 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167284 #edb/ref 1069 "q-340708" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167284 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167284 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=2$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ The Fourier sine series of the **odd** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the expression for ${b}_{n}.$\n\n$\\,{b}_{n}=$ {{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167284 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7a38bbe2-8cc7-5da0-8136-af259e1c0160" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167284 #edb/ref 1074 "For our Fourier sine series, the coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=2$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{n\\pi }[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{n\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{4(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi ))}{n\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )=(-1)^{n},$ we have\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{4(1-(-1)^{n})}{\\pi n}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167931 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167931 #edb/ref 1061 595576 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167931 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1dcd5d80-021e-5ab2-8637-bf6a5a9faf0e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167931 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186168039 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167932 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167933 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167932 #edb/ref 1061 595577 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167932 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "31f7c3df-5d68-5318-8629-44acb9dd6545" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167932 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186166955 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167933 #edb/ref 1061 595580 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167933 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "323c5600-b1d6-59c5-b7db-35b5ba7c1c94" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167933 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186166956 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168039 #edb/ref 1064 "In the earlier topics, we saw that *symmetry* can force many Fourier coefficients to be zero automatically.\nIn particular, if a $2\\pi$-periodic function is *odd*, then its Fourier series has *sine terms only*.\n\nA **Fourier sine series** uses this idea when a function is initially given only on the half-interval, say, $[0,\\pi),$ but we want a full Fourier series on $[-\\pi,\\pi)$ (and then a $2\\pi$-periodic extension to all real $x$).\n\nTo do this, we start with a function $F(x)$ on $[0,\\pi)$ and define its **odd extension** $f(x)$ to $(-\\pi,\\pi)$ by\n![](images/77/776119c1d430a7d50051ae0e00e254f9d22782bc192aa8a0cab9cc227fe460db.svg)\n\nFor example, a function $F(x),$ defined on $x \\in [0,1),$ and its odd extension $f(x)$ to $x \\in (-1,1)$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/2f/2f915ed450be1abf672e3fd86b56c02820514a778a4d4d174409f5bd99da6834.png)\n\nBy construction, $f$ is an *odd* function, meaning $f(-x)=-f(x)$. Because $f$ is odd, its Fourier series on $[-\\pi,\\pi)$ has *sine terms only*:\n\n$$\nf(x)\\sim \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} b_n\\sin(nx)\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is odd and $\\sin(nx)$ is odd for any $n \\geq 1,$ then $f(x)\\sin(nx)$ must be even.\nSo, the sine coefficients can be computed using the *half-range formula*:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot 2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo in practice, the workflow is the following:\n\n1\\. Start with $F$ on $[0,\\pi)$ and form the odd extension $f$ on $(-\\pi,\\pi).$\n\n2\\. Compute $b_n$ from the half-range integral on $[0,\\pi).$\n\n3\\. Write the Fourier sine series of $f.$\n\nLet's see some concrete examples." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168039 #edb/ref 1065 14652 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168039 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1e10dc0a-145b-598c-837b-7a6a2f858c85" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168039 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186312144 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{2}{n\\pi }((-1)^{n}(3\\pi +2)-2)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186312144 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186312144 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "17d2b4d4-59ed-5f0d-9922-89c02bc8942c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186312186 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{2}{n\\pi }((-1)^{n}(3\\pi -2)+2)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186312186 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186312186 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1c7dfd21-ea92-5be3-8cba-ebde5cac0a85" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186312201 #edb/ref 1080 "-2{x}^{3}+3x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186312201 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186312201 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1e3e4fe3-02d7-5d29-8ad7-3668f238c80b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186312316 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{n}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186312316 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186312316 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2bc2695f-0e15-5691-a028-b150d466dcf4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186312485 #edb/ref 1080 "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186312485 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186312485 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3f25da9e-c33a-5999-a8c4-ac447897722c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186312643 #edb/ref 1080 "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186312643 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186312643 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "525fe023-9b2c-5613-ac7a-b31e065a357b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186312667 #edb/ref 1080 "-6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1759218631