edb/ref 1071 "Let $F(x)=-2{x}^{3}+3x$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ and let $f(x)$ be the **odd** extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ What is $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315030 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9e12be66-5682-57ef-8004-80dc62d4ed67" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315030 #edb/ref 1074 "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ the odd extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}F(x), & x\\in [0,\\pi ), \\\\ -F(-x), & x\\in (-\\pi ,0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =-F(-x) \\\\  & =-(-2(-x)^{3}+3(-x)) \\\\  & =-(2{x}^{3}-3x) \\\\  & =-2{x}^{3}+3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315033 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314614 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315033 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314658 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315033 #edb/ref 1069 "q-340922" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315033 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315033 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $F(x)=-3x$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ Select the correct missing parts in the Fourier sine series of the **odd** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of the function $F(x)$ below.\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-1}}\\cdot {{field-2}}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315033 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7f36f74d-9ded-58b3-8942-de350905b5b9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315033 #edb/ref 1074 "For our Fourier sine series, the coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=-3x$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=-\\frac{6}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\, & ⇒\\,v=-\\frac{1}{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =-\\frac{6}{\\pi }(uv\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{\\pi }(-\\frac{1}{n}[x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }+\\frac{1}{n}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{n\\pi }((\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))-\\frac{1}{n}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{n\\pi }(\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-\\frac{1}{n}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )}{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )=(-1)^{n},$ we have\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{n}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the Fourier sine series is\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{n}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315064 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314558 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315064 #edb/ref 1069 "q-341210" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315064 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315064 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $F(x)=-{x}^{2}+5x$ for $x\\in [0,8),$ and let $f(x)$ be the **odd** extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in (-8,8).$ What is the value of $f(-3)?$\n\n$f(-3)=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315064 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c9a83be1-0157-5d64-aa36-c77e159ee194" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315064 #edb/ref 1074 "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [0,8),$ the odd extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}F(x), & x\\in [0,8), \\\\ -F(-x), & x\\in (-8,0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, for $x\\in (-8,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =-F(-x) \\\\  & =-(-(-x)^{2}+5(-x)) \\\\  & =-(-{x}^{2}-5x) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+5x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-3) & =(-3)^{2}+5(-3)=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379575 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186379576 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379575 #edb/ref 1069 "q-340895" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379575 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379575 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=3$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ The Fourier sine series of the **odd** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the expression for ${b}_{n}.$$\\,{b}_{n}=$ {{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379575 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f0702963-3f76-5783-ba13-4c9e296283ee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379575 #edb/ref 1074 "For our Fourier sine series, the coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=3$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{n\\pi }[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{n\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{6(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi ))}{n\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )=(-1)^{n},$ we have\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{6(1-(-1)^{n})}{\\pi n}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379576 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186379577 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379576 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186379578 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379576 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186379579 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379576 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186379580 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379576 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186379581 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379576 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379576 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592